@Tutorial(time: 10) {
    @Intro(title: "Configure Your First Socket") {
        Learn how a configuration describes a connection before any network activity begins. Start with a local checkout of NetworkLib, a Swift 6 toolchain, and the existing Examples target on macOS. See <doc:RunningExamples> for build requirements.
    }

    @Section(title: "Choose transport and security") {
        Follow the steps in order, then check your understanding below.

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                From the repository root, run `swift run Examples headers` to confirm that the existing CLI builds without needing a network service. Open the files under `Sources/Examples` in Xcode. The tutorial code files are snapshots for learning, not additional executable targets.
            }
            @Step {
                In a scratch Swift file in the existing Examples target, define `makeConfiguration()`. The hostname and port select the destination. The default transport is TCP and security is enabled. Creating a configuration does not open a connection.

                @Code(name: "Configuration.swift", file: "Configuration-01.swift")
            }
            @Step {
                Make the choices explicit. Keep `.any` to allow IPv4 or IPv6. Set TLS's server name to the destination hostname, limit each raw receive block to 16 KiB, and choose a 15-second inactivity timeout. The receive limit is not a limit on the full response size. Keep `interface: nil` to let the system select the network interface; this is also the default when the argument is omitted.

                @Code(name: "Configuration.swift", file: "Configuration-02.swift")
            }
            @Step {
                To require a specific network interface, import Network and accept an NWInterface in your helper. Pass it to the configuration's interface argument. You can obtain an interface from a path's availableInterfaces in an NWPathMonitor update. Choose the actual interface you intend to use; interface accepts an NWInterface instance, not a name such as en0 or an interface type such as .wifi.

                @Code(name: "Configuration.swift", file: "Configuration-03.swift")
            }
            @Step {
                The interface is a requirement, not a preference: NetworkLib assigns it to NWParameters.requiredInterface. The connection cannot fall back to another interface if the selected one is unavailable or cannot reach the destination. For the remaining lessons, use makeConfiguration() from the previous snapshot with interface: nil unless you specifically need this restriction.
            }
            @Step {
                Inspect the returned configuration in Xcode. For a local plaintext service, choose `isSecure: false` and that service's port. Selecting `.udp` with security enabled uses DTLS and requires a DTLS-capable peer. Continue with <doc:TransferBytes> to create and connect a socket.
            }
        }
    }

    @Assessments {
        @MultipleChoice {
            When does the network connection begin?

            @Choice(isCorrect: true) {
                When you call connect on a socket.

                @Justification {
                    The configuration describes the connection. A socket remains idle until connect starts it.
                }
            }

            @Choice(isCorrect: false) {
                When you create RawSocketConfiguration.

                @Justification {
                    The configuration describes the connection. A socket remains idle until connect starts it.
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
